{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\": \"R-17:33\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The focus’s تُعَذِّبَ is a discretionary punishment; 17:33 offers a related Quranic limit, forbidding excess even when a victim’s heir has authority to seek redress. This makes restraint a relevant comparison, not a stated rule for Dhu al-Qarnayn.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"17:33\", \"ar\": \"فَقَدْ جَعَلْنَا لِوَلِيِّهِۦ سُلْطَٰنًۭا فَلَا يُسْرِف فِّى ٱلْقَتْلِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus places punishment among the ruler’s options.\", \"limits\": \"17:33 concerns retaliation for wrongful killing; the focus does not specify that offense.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-17:34\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"Both passages pair a limit marked by بَلَغَ with an ethical duty toward others: 17:34 permits only what is best regarding an orphan’s property until maturity, while the focus follows arrival with the option to take حُسْنًا among a people.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"17:34\", \"ar\": \"إِلَّا بِٱلَّتِى هِىَ أَحْسَنُ حَتَّىٰ يَبْلُغَ أَشُدَّهُۥ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus’s arrival opens a choice about conduct toward a people.\", \"limits\": \"17:34 addresses guardianship of an orphan’s wealth; it does not define Dhu al-Qarnayn’s authority.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-17:86\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:14\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:17\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Surah 18 already presents sunset in relation to a human setting: the sun at the cave passes to the sleepers’ left, while the focus reports Dhu al-Qarnayn finding it setting at a spring. The paired scenes make the focus’s wording part of a narrated encounter with terrain and a traveler’s vantage.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:17\", \"ar\": \"وَإِذَا غَرَبَت تَّقْرِضُهُمْ ذَاتَ ٱلشِّمَالِ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages describe the sun’s setting within a particular scene.\", \"limits\": \"The cave episode does not settle whether the focus describes appearance, geography, or both.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:36\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:49\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:50\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The focus uses تَتَّخِذَ for taking goodness among the people; 18:50 uses the same verb for taking Iblis and his descendants as protectors, which is condemned. The contrast gives the verb a moral-choice resonance within the surah.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:50\", \"ar\": \"أَفَتَتَّخِذُونَهُۥ وَذُرِّيَّتَهُۥٓ أَوْلِيَآءَ مِن دُونِى\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus’s second option is framed with the verb تَتَّخِذَ.\", \"limits\": \"The surah does not equate taking goodness among people with taking protectors; the contrast concerns different objects and acts.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:53\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:60\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"Moses’s resolve to travel until he reaches the meeting of the two seas echoes the focus’s حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ journey frame. Across the surah, reaching a destination marks a transition into a new encounter or event.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:60\", \"ar\": \"لَآ أَبْرَحُ حَتَّىٰٓ أَبْلُغَ مَجْمَعَ ٱلْبَحْرَيْنِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The repeated حتى and بَلَغَ construction connects the journeys.\", \"limits\": \"Moses’s destination and purpose differ from Dhu al-Qarnayn’s itinerary.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:61\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"At the meeting of the seas, reaching a place is followed by a surprising event involving the fish’s path through the sea; in the focus, reaching the sun’s setting place is followed by finding the sun and a people. The shared journey pattern makes both arrivals scene-opening thresholds.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:61\", \"ar\": \"فَلَمَّا بَلَغَا مَجْمَعَ بَيْنِهِمَا نَسِيَا حُوتَهُمَا فَٱتَّخَذَ سَبِيلَهُۥ فِى ٱلْبَحْرِ سَرَبًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"Both accounts link a reached boundary or meeting place with a narrated discovery.\", \"limits\": \"The fish episode is not a direct parallel to the solar scene, and its water setting does not identify the spring in the focus.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:71\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:76\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"In 18:76, بَلَغْتَ marks reaching an excuse that closes the exchange between Moses and his companion; the focus uses بَلَغَ for reaching a geographic limit that opens a new encounter. The root can mark a consequential boundary in both scenes.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:76\", \"ar\": \"قَدْ بَلَغْتَ مِن لَّدُنِّى عُذْرًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The shared verb بَلَغَ marks a limit in each passage.\", \"limits\": \"One limit is geographic and begins an episode; the other is interpersonal and ends one.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:82\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The preceding Moses account ends with the servant saying وَمَا فَعَلْتُهُۥ عَنْ أَمْرِى; the focus then makes Dhu al-Qarnayn’s discretion explicit through divine speech, قُلْنَا … إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ. The juxtaposition distinguishes an action whose divine basis is disclosed afterward from a ruler’s options stated in advance.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:82\", \"ar\": \"وَمَا فَعَلْتُهُۥ عَنْ أَمْرِى\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus directly introduces Dhu al-Qarnayn’s choice with قُلْنَا.\", \"limits\": \"The passages do not say that the two figures hold the same office or receive the same kind of instruction.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:83\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus’s journey is the first scene in the account announced by the question about ذِى ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ. The preceding call to recite his account frames the encounter and choice as part of a reported narrative, not an isolated ruling.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:83\", \"ar\": \"وَيَسْـَٔلُونَكَ عَن ذِى ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ ۖ قُلْ سَأَتْلُوا۟ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْهُ ذِكْرًا\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The title in the focus directly answers the preceding account’s subject.\", \"limits\": \"The announcement does not explain the details of the western encounter or its judgment.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:84\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The account first says God established Dhu al-Qarnayn in the land and gave him means; the focus then shows him traveling to an endpoint and being offered authority over a people. His reach and discretion are presented within divinely granted capacity.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:84\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّا مَكَّنَّا لَهُۥ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ مِن كُلِّ شَىْءٍۢ سَبَبًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus’s journey and delegated choice follow the account of his establishment and means.\", \"limits\": \"The passage does not specify which particular means he used to reach the western endpoint.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:85\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The brief statement فَأَتْبَعَ سَبَبًا is carried into the focus’s first narrated destination: حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ marks where following a means brings him, and وَجَدَ opens the encounter that follows.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:85\", \"ar\": \"فَأَتْبَعَ سَبَبًا\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus immediately continues the itinerary introduced by فَأَتْبَعَ سَبَبًا.\", \"limits\": \"The focus names neither the particular means nor the full route.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:87\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The next verse specifies the punishment option تُعَذِّبَ: Dhu al-Qarnayn says he will punish whoever ظَلَمَ, then that person will return to his Lord for a further punishment. The response limits his stated action to wrongdoers and distinguishes it from the Lord’s later judgment.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:87\", \"ar\": \"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ ثُمَّ يُرَدُّ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِۦ فَيُعَذِّبُهُۥ عَذَابًۭا نُّكْرًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The immediate reply takes up the focus’s first branch and names its condition.\", \"limits\": \"The passage does not detail the form or procedure of Dhu al-Qarnayn’s punishment.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:88\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus’s option حُسْنًا is developed as a response to whoever believes and acts rightly: that person receives ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ and is told of ease from Dhu al-Qarnayn’s authority. Good treatment here is one branch of a differentiated judgment, not an undivided offer to everyone.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:88\", \"ar\": \"وَأَمَّا مَنْ ءَامَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَٰلِحًۭا فَلَهُۥ جَزَآءً ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ ۖ وَسَنَقُولُ لَهُۥ مِنْ أَمْرِنَا يُسْرًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The next verse repeats the goodness root in ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ and identifies who receives this branch.\", \"limits\": \"The reply does not define every possible form of حُسْنًا in the focus.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:89\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"After the two judgments, ثُمَّ أَتْبَعَ سَبَبًا resumes the itinerary. The western encounter is one stage in a continuing sequence, not the account’s endpoint.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:89\", \"ar\": \"ثُمَّ أَتْبَعَ سَبَبًا\"}], \"activation\": \"The verse resumes movement directly after the focus’s encounter and its two responses.\", \"limits\": \"This transition adds no further detail about how Dhu al-Qarnayn governs the western people.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:90\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The eastern journey repeats the focus’s structure: after following a means, Dhu al-Qarnayn reaches a solar limit and finds the sun and a people. The west has the sun setting at a spring and a people nearby; the east has it rising upon a people without shelter. Their parallel frames the focus as one of paired encounters while preserving the difference between them.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:90\", \"ar\": \"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَطْلِعَ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا تَطْلُعُ عَلَىٰ قَوْمٍۢ لَّمْ نَجْعَل لَّهُم مِّن دُونِهَا سِتْرًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The repeated arrival, finding, solar reference, and people link the paired scenes.\", \"limits\": \"The eastern passage does not repeat the focus’s spring or the explicit choice between punishment and goodness.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:91\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"After the eastern solar encounter, the narration says God has encompassed what Dhu al-Qarnayn possesses or knows. This divine frame places the focus’s report of what he وَجَدَهَا within a story whose full scope is known to God, without treating the traveler’s perspective as exhaustive.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:91\", \"ar\": \"كَذَٰلِكَ وَقَدْ أَحَطْنَا بِمَا لَدَيْهِ خُبْرًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus reports what Dhu al-Qarnayn finds; the following journey’s frame states God’s encompassing knowledge of what is with him.\", \"limits\": \"18:91 does not explicitly correct or explain the focus’s description of the sun.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:92\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The third movement begins with another ثُمَّ أَتْبَعَ سَبَبًا before a new arrival among a people. Like the focus, that arrival belongs to a recurring itinerary structure in which travel leads to a distinct encounter.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"وَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:92\", \"ar\": \"ثُمَّ أَتْبَعَ سَبَبًا\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus is the first encounter in a sequence resumed by the same movement formula before the next one.\", \"limits\": \"The transition does not imply that the later people receive the same treatment as the western group.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:93\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"At the third endpoint, the focus’s pattern of reaching a place and finding a people recurs. The western scene gives no account of the people’s speech; at the barriers, Dhu al-Qarnayn encounters people who can scarcely understand speech, and their different situation leads into a different appeal.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:93\", \"ar\": \"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ بَيْنَ ٱلسَّدَّيْنِ وَوَجَدَ مِن دُونِهِمَا قَوْمًۭا لَّا يَكَادُونَ يَفْقَهُونَ قَوْلًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages use the arrival-and-finding pattern, with a people at each destination.\", \"limits\": \"The focus does not say that its people share the later group’s difficulty understanding speech.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:94\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The people at the barriers address Dhu al-Qarnayn directly and offer payment for protection against فساد. This contrasts with the focus, where he finds a people and receives a divine statement of possible responses before any request from them is reported.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"وَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا ۗ قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:94\", \"ar\": \"قَالُوا۟ يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِنَّ يَأْجُوجَ وَمَأْجُوجَ مُفْسِدُونَ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ فَهَلْ نَجْعَلُ لَكَ خَرْجًا\"}], \"activation\": \"Later in the same account, another people’s appeal makes their need and proposed exchange explicit.\", \"limits\": \"The focus’s silence about a request does not prove that the western people made none.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:95\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"In the barrier episode, Dhu al-Qarnayn says what his Lord has enabled him with is better than payment, then asks the people to help with their strength. This develops the focus’s delegated authority as capacity used with communal participation.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:95\", \"ar\": \"قَالَ مَا مَكَّنِّى فِيهِ رَبِّى خَيْرٌۭ فَأَعِينُونِى بِقُوَّةٍ أَجْعَلْ بَيْنَكُمْ وَبَيْنَهُمْ رَدْمًا\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus introduces an authority to respond to a people; the later scene shows another exercise of that authority.\", \"limits\": \"The barrier project addresses a different threat and does not specify how the western people were treated.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:96\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The account later shows Dhu al-Qarnayn directing the construction of a barrier through ordered instructions. This supplies a concrete instance of his practical authority after the focus has presented him as a ruler entrusted with a choice.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:96\", \"ar\": \"ءَاتُونِى زُبَرَ ٱلْحَدِيدِ ۖ حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا سَاوَىٰ بَيْنَ ٱلصَّدَفَيْنِ قَالَ ٱنفُخُوا۟\"}], \"activation\": \"The same figure later directs collective work in a separate encounter.\", \"limits\": \"The construction scene does not clarify the criteria for his first choice.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:97\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The later account says the people cannot climb over the barrier or breach it. That outcome shows the protective effectiveness of Dhu al-Qarnayn’s subsequent work, alongside the earlier scene of authority to respond to a people.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:97\", \"ar\": \"فَمَا ٱسْطَٰعُوٓا۟ أَن يَظْهَرُوهُ وَمَا ٱسْتَطَٰعُوا۟ لَهُۥ نَقْبًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"Both scenes belong to Dhu al-Qarnayn’s encounters with communities and his exercise of authority.\", \"limits\": \"The barrier’s success does not explain the western judgment or its outcome.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:98\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"After the barrier succeeds, Dhu al-Qarnayn calls it mercy from his Lord and says it will be flattened when the Lord’s promise comes. This qualifies the focus’s delegated choice: his authority and works sit within divine provision and a larger limit.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:98\", \"ar\": \"قَالَ هَٰذَا رَحْمَةٌۭ مِّن رَّبِّى ۖ فَإِذَا جَآءَ وَعْدُ رَبِّى جَعَلَهُۥ دَكَّآءَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The same account moves from a divinely stated choice to Dhu al-Qarnayn’s explicit attribution of later success to his Lord.\", \"limits\": \"18:98 speaks about the barrier and its eventual end, not directly about the western judgment.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-18:99\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The passage proceeds from the barrier’s promised destruction to the upheaval and gathering of the Last Day. This places Dhu al-Qarnayn’s work, and by extension the authority shown in the focus, within a history that reaches its divinely set end.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:98\", \"ar\": \"فَإِذَا جَآءَ وَعْدُ رَبِّى جَعَلَهُۥ دَكَّآءَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"18:99\", \"ar\": \"وَنُفِخَ فِى ٱلصُّورِ فَجَمَعْنَٰهُمْ جَمْعًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The account of Dhu al-Qarnayn ends by naming the Lord’s promise before the gathering scene.\", \"limits\": \"This transition does not describe the eventual consequences of the focus’s particular judgment.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-19:8\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The focus uses بَلَغَ for reaching a place; 19:8 uses the same root for reaching extreme old age. The comparison shows the root’s range from spatial arrival to a bodily limit, without joining the two stories.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"19:8\", \"ar\": \"وَقَدْ بَلَغْتُ مِنَ ٱلْكِبَرِ عِتِيًّۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus’s journey hinges on the verb بَلَغَ.\", \"limits\": \"Zachariah’s question about old age supplies no narrative or ethical parallel to Dhu al-Qarnayn’s encounter.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-19:75\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"Both passages use إِمَّا … وَإِمَّا to frame alternatives. In the focus these are actions available to Dhu al-Qarnayn; in 19:75 they are threatened outcomes for the misguided. The shared form highlights the difference in who acts and what the alternatives do.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"19:75\", \"ar\": \"إِمَّا ٱلْعَذَابَ وَإِمَّا ٱلسَّاعَةَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The repeated disjunction links the wording, while the surrounding clauses assign it different roles.\", \"limits\": \"The threat in 19:75 does not define the ruler’s choices in the focus.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-20:44\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"Moses and Aaron are commanded to speak gently even to Pharaoh, who is said to have transgressed. This gives a cross-surah example of gentleness toward a wrongdoer that resonates with the focus’s option of حُسْنًا, without deciding when that option applies.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"20:44\", \"ar\": \"فَقُولَا لَهُۥ قَوْلًۭا لَّيِّنًۭا لَّعَلَّهُۥ يَتَذَكَّرُ أَوْ يَخْشَىٰ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus sets out goodness as an alternative to punishment; the parallel gives gentleness as a commanded mode of address.\", \"limits\": \"The passages do not equate the people in the focus with Pharaoh or define حُسْنًا as gentle speech alone.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-20:51\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-20:65\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The magicians’ إِمَّآ أَن … وَإِمَّآ أَن construction formally matches the focus’s two-part choice. Its contents are a contest over who casts first, so the parallel shows the form can frame alternatives without making the scenes or their stakes equivalent.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"20:65\", \"ar\": \"إِمَّآ أَن تُلْقِىَ وَإِمَّآ أَن نَّكُونَ أَوَّلَ مَنْ أَلْقَىٰ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages pair إِمَّا with أَنْ and a subjunctive verb in each branch.\", \"limits\": \"The contest’s procedural choice does not clarify Dhu al-Qarnayn’s ethical alternatives.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-20:68\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-20:116\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-20:130\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The focus makes the sun’s setting a destination in Dhu al-Qarnayn’s itinerary; 20:130 names sunset as a time before which to praise God. Together they show a shared solar marker used in different ways: a travel scene here, a devotional time there.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ\"}, {\"ref\": \"20:130\", \"ar\": \"قَبْلَ طُلُوعِ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَقَبْلَ غُرُوبِهَا\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages foreground the sun’s setting, but embed it in different actions.\", \"limits\": \"The devotional timing does not identify the focus’s destination or resolve its description of the spring.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-21:15\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-21:53\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-21:69\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-21:78\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"David and Solomon judge a dispute involving damage to a people’s crops, with God witnessing their judgment. This offers a Quranic ruler-judgment parallel to the focus’s delegated choice about how to respond to a people.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا ۗ قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"21:78\", \"ar\": \"إِذْ يَحْكُمَانِ فِى ٱلْحَرْثِ إِذْ نَفَشَتْ فِيهِ غَنَمُ ٱلْقَوْمِ وَكُنَّا لِحُكْمِهِمْ شَٰهِدِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages place a ruler’s judgment in relation to a community’s circumstances.\", \"limits\": \"The focus does not name the western people’s offense or present a comparable dispute over property.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-21:79\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The account of David and Solomon says God gave both judgment and knowledge, while the focus shows Dhu al-Qarnayn receiving an explicit choice from divine speech. Together they present human judgment as operating within a divine grant or instruction.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"18:86\", \"ar\": \"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}, {\"ref\": \"21:79\", \"ar\": \"وَكُلًّا ءَاتَيْنَا حُكْمًۭا وَعِلْمًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus’s divine address grants alternatives; 21:79 explicitly attributes judgment and knowledge to God’s gift.\", \"limits\": \"The verses describe different figures and do not establish that their authorities or cases are identical.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-23:32\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}"}}
